{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1350690-breeds-yet-unknown","id":1350690,"title":"Breeds Yet Unknown","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-11-24T12:40:17-08:00","id":1350690,"title":"Breeds Yet Unknown","content":"Heya, so just a curious question that came to mind, but if y'all could add any new changing breeds, even in the form of lost breeds in the settings past, what would they be?<br /> <br /> What sort of animal would you add to the toolbox and what sort of role would you have that changing breed fill? I'm curious to here anything from raw concepts to fully realized homebrew, so hit the thread with whatever you like.<br /> <br /> Also, alternatively, if given the power you wouldn't create any new changing breeds, why not? No judgement, I'm just curious as to your reasoning.<br /> <br /> Anyway, thank y'all anyway for reading this far and good trails to all y'all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-24T15:56:54-08:00","id":1350718,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't add any Changing Breeds, and honestly think there's too many in the game as is. This is one area where the game is prone to shift too much into fantasy instead of gothic horror (which should be based on real world myths, legends, and folktales).<br /> <br /> In the real world, shapechanging folklore is generally limited to whatever animal is the most dangerous predator in the area and is known to kill livestock and even humans. In those places, people have stories of witchcraft where malevolent people know magic that can turn them into such creatures to harm their neighbors. In much of the world, these are wolves. But where wolves don't exist, it's tigers, jaguars, hyenas, etc. Those are fine, but the game already has all of them that I know of.<br /> <br /> Outside of these stories, you get examples of fairy tales where spirits or fairy creatures can take the form of animals or people. Unlike the above, these are not human - they are magical creatures. And you gets stories of foxes, seals, swans, etc. Most of these belong to the &quot;animal brides&quot; genre of folktales. I'd prefer these to not be Changing Breeds, but belong to Changeling and Faerie.<br /> <br /> Things like wererats, werespiders, and wereboars, as far as I know, originate from fantasy, and aren't really supported by real world folklore. They shouldn't be in the game. However, I am fine with there being prey animals of the Changing Breed type (like wereboars) provided they were all slain in the ancient past like in the War of Rage. It's a good compromise between certain aspects of the game and actual folklore. If they died off early enough, it explains why we don't have actual legends about them.<br /> <br /> Then there are nebulous things like werebears and weresharks, which I'm uncertain if they are represented in folklore, but I allow in. The Gurahl are OK because there is a lot of evident of primitive bear-cults where bears were once worshiped. And I think that's close enough to allow something in the game. And I allow the Rokea because Polynesia should be represented somehow, and there isn't any local land predator that can fill the role. Mokole are OK, but only as werecrocodiles - I don't use any of this dinosaur stuff.<br /> <br /> So I can't think of any animal that is represented in real world folklore, but isn't in the game. It's possible someone else will post something, and I'll smack my forehead for not thinking about it. We'll see. But in general, there are only existing breeds in game I'd remove. And in my chronicles, I usually do a form of selective editing of drop certain Changing Breeds and never mention them. I'm not entirely consistent. I keep wereravens in the game although they clearly don't fit with my scope's definition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-24T16:54:14-08:00","id":1350733,"title":"","content":"That's all very fair and certainly a solid reason and justification for not having those elements be present in the setting to fit into the thematic presentation of the Gothic-Punk setting that the World of Darkness strives for.<br /> <br /> Out of curiosity, given your feelings on the matter, how do you tend to feel about the in-universe/in-character justification for each changing breed, in that they all performed some Gaian duty in the legendary era o pre-history that helped ensure the balance of life.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-24T19:17:23-08:00","id":1350753,"title":"","content":"I used to think Ratkin were pure fantasy then found out about this whole rat thing but there's SOME basis on it from Japanese Folklore. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesso\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesso</a><br /> <br /> Same with Boars<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twrch_Trwyth\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Detail_of_Twrch_Trwyth_sculpture_-_geograph.org.uk_-_915217.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twrch_Trwyth\" target=\"_blank\"> Twrch Trwyth - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twrch_Trwyth\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twrch_Trwyth </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Boars are pretty much the murder machine of heroes in celtic myth.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Spiders and to a lesser extent Nagah seem to be more readily based on &quot;turns into a giant scary version of..&quot; which seems pretty common around the world that being the case I'd probably add a True Bird of Prey as the lack of any other birds feels like a missed opportunity. And having Rocs as a Breed would be pretty awesome and fear of Giant birds like giant spiders and snakes is a primal one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T08:59:34-08:00","id":1350835,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1350690-breeds-yet-unknown?p=1350733#post1350733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Out of curiosity, given your feelings on the matter, how do you tend to feel about the in-universe/in-character justification for each changing breed, in that they all performed some Gaian duty in the legendary era o pre-history that helped ensure the balance of life.</div> </div> </div> </div>When I run NPCs, I have them absolutely be convinced that is the case. In my head as ST, I know it is only a Just So Story that gives them a sense of purpose. It's pure mythology. And that's OK.<br /> <br /> It is an example of something that sounds good, but actually falls apart when you look at it too closely. The different purposes only work if all of these creatures occupy the same land more or less. But for the most part they don't. So for long stretches of time, only some of those purposes could be fulfilled in that part of the world. So did Gaia simply not intend that function X not be performed there?<br /> <br /> I think it is possible that in a very loose sense, that broad categories of Fera (predators vs prey, beasts vs birds, etc.) have a &quot;purpose&quot; in the way their mundane animal counterparts fit an ecological role. And probably more in a spiritual archetype sense than in their animal's ecological role. But nothing narrowly defined.<br /> <br /> I do think all of them are &quot;defenders of the land&quot;. And they each have gifts that correspond to their spiritual nature, and so some of them fit better roles than others. But in general many of them can fill multiple roles themselves - just not as good as others could do.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T10:01:40-08:00","id":1350842,"title":"","content":"As much as I like all of the Fera to some degree or another, in perfect hindsight and in interest of less setting &quot;clutter&quot;, one can argue that there should probably only be five other breeds in addition to werewolves: leopard, tiger, hyena, jaguar, and shark, filling the Garou niche in places where there are few if any wolves. (Also, argueably, only seven tribes.) However, in such a setting, I'd also argue for the inclusion of a separate race/teir of Tricksters, with their own breeds of Snake, Raven, Coyote, Monkey, Hare, Fox, and maybe Spider. <br /> <br /> One the other hand, I've always thought were-cephalopods would be interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T10:30:30-08:00","id":1350850,"title":"","content":"That's all very fair stuff and I can certainly see the logic of thinning out the setting to keep it more trimmed and honed for the themes and moods that you're going after.<br /> <br /> Although in the interest of conversation, I've got a few more questions to throw out.<br /> <br /> Given your position Black Fox, which is certainly fair, what's your opinion of the concept proposed by Shattered Dreams (even if it's only quasi-canon) that in the arguably mythic golden age of the Changing Breeds, the Breeds were often working in cooperation with each other with each filling their own role and niche in the defense and support of Gaia?<br /> <br /> Also to overcome my own mortal dread and unapologetic distrust of cephalopods, what's your vision N.O.O.C. for what were-cephalopods would look like as a gaian changing breed? I imagine there interactions with the Rokea would be interesting given the sharks equate their vision of the Wyrm with a squid iirc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T12:24:34-08:00","id":1350879,"title":"","content":"I haven't read Shattered Breeds and am unlikely to ever read or use it because what little I know about it, it seems to go against certain decisions I made early on about the setting in my chronicles. I can answer your question, but will do it in a roundabout way.<br /> <br /> I have a set &quot;start&quot; period of history in my WoD of 12,000 BC. It is when the Ice Age ended and the Pleistocene became the Holocene. By that time, mankind had spread to all areas of the globe. That is when Gaia created all the Changing Breeds. All the Garou, Bastet, Corax, etc. came into being. Before that time, only the &quot;world before&quot; existed. That was the antedeluvial world, the previous root race of Theosophy, Conan's Hyborian Age, Graham Hancock's Ice Age Civilization, or whatever you want to call it existed and then fell. Since we are talking about a time fundamentally different than now, the legacy of that existed in the Umbra, not the material Realm. So that is why there really isn't traces of it. Only when that &quot;world&quot; ended and the &quot;new&quot; one was born did Gaia see the need for the Changing Breeds. (One problem about setting the Fera in the really far past, is that until humans live in lands where certain animals exist, that Changing Breed can't exist either. So werejaguars, for example, have to be a young species created far after the Garou, Khan, etc. I don't like multiple origins of the Changing Breeds, or the implications of certain breeds being older than others. So I need a &quot;start time&quot; when all breeds can come into play.) From what I understand, Shattered Dreams extends way too far past that date for it to ever be useful to me.<br /> <br /> However, at the same time, I do have The Time Before Time. This is essentially a mythic time of animal spirits which is found in Garou legends. So all that &quot;happened&quot;, of course this being the spirit realm and not the material realm, there are all sorts of myths - all equally true, and many contradicting each other. But all the Changing Breeds came after this.<br /> <br /> Of course, for a long time after the creation of the Changing Breeds, the Gauntlet was either very weak or non-existent. So the spirit realm and material realm were essentially the same place. So you have things like Luna creating auspices, Falcon talking to Helios, etc. This is generally the time when the first Pacts were created and the first tribes formed. It was also a time when the last vestiges of Chaos - Wyrm corrupted Wyld spirits like the Volag/Fomorians/Jotuns and the great Dragons/Zmei - were around. Lots of other monsters as well. <br /> <br /> So there were lots for the Changing Breeds to do. So that could be considered a Golden Age of Fera cooperation. But at the same time, that is a very alien world than is now since &quot;spirit logic&quot; works and doesn't fit the rational mind people, including the Changing Breeds, use nowadays. It doesn't fit easily into the &quot;history&quot; we know of the real world from archaeology, paleontology, etc. because the metaphysics of the world back then was different from what we know now. But there probably is evidence you can find in the Pangea Near Realm and other places. <br /> <br /> &quot;History&quot; before the Gauntlet is a very nebulous thing. So while I insist that at the Impergium, none of the Garou tribes in Eurasia had ever heard of the Wendigo, Croatan, or Uktena. I also don't have a problem saying those tribes were also present when the Garou established/received the first version of the Litany at Table Rock thousands of years earlier. It's just that Table Rock is now at Pangea somewhere (and was likewise always in the Umbra).<br /> <br /> As the Gauntlet took hold, and there were less common enemies to fight, the different Changing Breeds of course began to compete with each other over resources (kinfolk, caerns, etc.). But all the various fighting was local and sporadic. There wasn't one single event or cause or orchestrating group. In one area of the world, all the Changing Breeds may have been killing each other, but in another, they were working very closely against a local, immediate threat.<br /> <br /> I view the Garou and their septs as to be the equivalent of the old Greek City States in the Iliad and such. They are prone to fight each other, but have a sense of &quot;us&quot; that separates them from the barbarians (everyone else). So they can unite when needed. The Fera are likewise, and all the Changing Breeds acknowledge some loose kinship with each other, but not too much. So their tendency to cooperate or fight each other depends on lots of things.<br /> <br /> In general, as you reach farther back in time when the Realm and Umbra were one, &quot;spirit logic&quot; of mythic times take hold. Individual Garou are much less likely to be &quot;people&quot; as we know them, and more likely to act like archetypes. All the Gaian forces work in harmony to fight the Wyrm. As spirit from the material separates, that fairy tale logic is no longer able to keep the breeds or tribes together.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T12:35:15-08:00","id":1350883,"title":"","content":"Gotcha, very cool stuff. That's an interesting way to look at it and I'll give it some thought about crimping parts for my own usage if that's cool.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T13:59:52-08:00","id":1350904,"title":"","content":"I rather like that set up. Not least because it plays well with two unrelated ideas I've had over the years; One being that Three Brothers have their own origin myth about Gaia creating them in the Pure Lands to protect the Pure Lands (with this land bridge thing being a white man fiction), and the other that there was a time before the gauntet when spirits were everywhere, animals talked, nothing really died, true fey did as they pleased, and the things that would eventually become mage avatars oversaw the tapistry that underpins reality. Then something happened to break that (probably whatever it was that punched the hole in reality that is Oblivion), and Gaia eventually created the various shifters to try to do damage control.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}